WHOISleuth is a defensive domain investigation and brand-protection tool. Its
command-line package can collect bounded WHOIS, RDAP, DNS, HTTP, TLS,
certificate-transparency, website, and related infrastructure evidence. It can
also generate and review lookalike-domain candidates, compare saved evidence,
and prepare analyst-controlled reports. These security-relevant capabilities
can have both legitimate and potentially harmful uses.

WHOISleuth is intended for authorised defensive investigation, abuse response,
threat research, domain acquisition due diligence, asset review, and education.
Network collection is initiated explicitly by the operator, uses bounded
requests, and keeps source provenance and incomplete results visible. Hosted
and distributable collection does not exploit targets, bypass access controls,
submit credentials, execute remote page scripts, or automate enforcement
actions. The separate repo-local rendered-capture package is an explicit
authorised exception: it executes page JavaScript in a disposable,
network-bounded browser and writes only a local screenshot plus sanitised
digest and manifest artefacts for operator-controlled review and deletion.

Operators are responsible for using WHOISleuth only on systems and data they
are authorised to examine and in accordance with applicable laws, provider
terms, and organisational policies. Observations and heuristic signals are
investigative leads, not proof of ownership, intent, safety, or maliciousness.
